{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 28, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending as a new statute reinstates penalties for carrying small quantities of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth. Under HB4002, individuals found with these drugs can face a misdemeanor charge with up to six months in jail—or opt into a behavioral health pathway designed to steer them away from incarceration. The law also tightens restrictions on selling drugs near areas like playgrounds and schools, signaling a stronger enforcement stance.\n\n***\n\nThe legislation promotes, but does not require, local jurisdictions to establish alternatives that connect people with support services. While some see this flexibility as practical, others warn it could lead to uneven outcomes across regions. Police are instructed to offer a diversion route only if specific conditions are met: no prior offenses, no outstanding warrants, no signs of aggression, and no medical emergencies. If these criteria aren’t satisfied, arrest is the default.\n\n***\n\nWhen eligible, officers contact a regional hotline to initiate a rapid response. A mobile health team must arrive within half an hour to assess and engage the individual. If they don’t show up in time, the person will be taken into custody. Officers are under no obligation to linger; the window for intervention is tight, and the system prioritizes immediate action over prolonged negotiation.\n\n***\n\nThis shift reflects a broader national conversation about how to respond to substance use. In this country, approaches vary widely, and many wonder how to balance accountability with compassion. One might ask: what does it mean to truly help someone who’s struggling? Is it about punishment, or about being there when they’re ready to change? Maybe the real question is how often we’ve mistaken silence for strength—how many have gone without care because no one knew how to listen. You can’t eat your way out of pain, nor shop your way to peace. But sometimes, just hearing someone’s tongue speak their truth is the first step.\n\nP.P.S Age doesn’t erase pain, and no country has all the answers. You can’t eat your way out of trauma, nor shop your way to healing—but a kind word, a listening tongue, might just be the lifeline someone needs.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.554117739200592}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -8.904537389753386e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.31348296999931335}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.007826023735105991}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.24009528756141663}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.9124716520309448}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.03640199452638626}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.03628002479672432}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.050031621009111404}, {"token": " reinst", "logprob": -1.1101841926574707}, {"token": "ates", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.5083760619163513}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " carrying", "logprob": -0.5961156487464905}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.00213300040923059}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -0.07987315952777863}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.05112536624073982}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -0.00015937011630740017}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.003980927634984255}, {"token"